At first, some scientists suggested a [[meteor|meteor]] had exploded high over Kerala, leaving red dust in the air. People in one area reported a loud boom before the first red rain.

But when researchers looked at the particles closely, they didn't look like dust. They looked like living cells, with thick walls. Dust from a meteor wouldn't look like that.

{{story:alien-life}}

At the very centre sits a supermassive [[@black-holes|black hole]] called Sagittarius A*. It has about 4 million times the mass of the Sun. Two teams, led by Reinhard Genzel in Germany and Andrea Ghez in the USA, tracked stars whipping around it for decades. Their work won the 2020 Nobel Prize in Physics.

In 2022, the Event Horizon Telescope, a team using radio dishes across the world as one giant telescope, released the first picture of the glowing ring around it.

{{story:ladakh}}

Dimorphos is about 160 m across, roughly as wide as a cricket stadium. It orbits a bigger asteroid called Didymos. Neither was ever a threat, which made them a safe target. On 26 September 2022, the DART spacecraft, about the size of a small car, slammed into Dimorphos at about 22,000 km/h.

Before the crash, Dimorphos took 11 hours and 55 minutes to circle Didymos. Afterwards it took about 32 minutes less. The impact blasted out a huge plume of rock and dust, and that spray pushed the asteroid even harder than the spacecraft itself. The full story is told in [[@dart|DART]].

{{story:dart}}

A spacecraft in [[orbit|orbit]] races around Earth at about 28,000 km an hour. When it dives back into the air, the air in front of it gets squashed and rubbed, and the outside heats up to thousands of degrees. You can feel a tiny version of this heat from [[@friction|friction]] by rubbing your palms together fast.

The crew module has a heat shield on its base to protect the people inside. The service module has none. So it is let go and burns up, while the capsule slows down, opens its [[@parachutes|parachutes]] and splashes into the sea. NASA's [[@apollo-moon|Apollo]] astronauts came home from the Moon in exactly the same way in 1969 and the years after.

{{story:gaganyaan}}

A pet's daily routine matters most. Dogs and cats should be fed proper pet food in the right amounts, because many human foods are harmful to them. [[@chocolate-making|Chocolate]], grapes, onions and garlic can make dogs ill. Fresh water should be available all day, especially in hot Indian summers.

Dogs need walks and play every day. Cats need scratching posts, places to hide and a clean litter tray. Small pets like rabbits and guinea pigs need space to run, things to chew and company of their own kind. Fish tanks need a filter, regular partial water changes and a careful number of fish. Too many fish in a small tank quickly fouls the water, however often it is cleaned. A good rule is to set up the tank and let the filter run for a few weeks before adding fish.

{{story:kindness-science}}

Early court ballets were performed by nobles, not professionals. They wore heavy costumes, masks and high-heeled shoes, so the steps were slow and graceful rather than athletic.

The young Louis XIV took part in many ballets. In 1653, aged 14, he danced the role of the rising Sun in a ballet that lasted all night. The part gave him his famous nickname, the Sun King. His dancing master, Pierre Beauchamp, is credited with describing the five basic positions of the feet that every ballet student still learns.

{{story:renaissance}}

Colours mean different things in different places. In China and much of India, [[@colour-meaning|red]] is the colour of luck and joy, so brides wear red saris, lehengas or silk jackets. In parts of East Asia, white was long worn for mourning.

In Britain, Queen Victoria married Prince Albert in a white satin dress in 1840. Pictures of it spread, and rich brides copied her. Over the next century, white became the classic Western wedding colour. Before that, many brides wore their best dress in blue, grey or any colour.

{{story:colour-meaning}}

Among the Hadza people of Tanzania, who still hunt and gather, a big animal like an antelope is shared through the whole camp. The hunter doesn't get to keep most of it. Anthropologists see this as a kind of insurance. Today I share with you; next week, when I fail, you share with me.

It also builds reputation. People who share are trusted, chosen as partners and helped when they need it. People who never share find others less willing to help them. In many hunter-gatherer groups, boasting about a big catch is frowned upon, which keeps successful hunters humble.

{{story:game-theory}}

The hardest argument was about power. Big states wanted seats in Congress based on population. Small states wanted every state to count equally.

The answer, called the Great Compromise, gave Congress two parts. In the House of Representatives, bigger states get more seats. In the Senate, every state gets two senators. That balance still shapes how the president is chosen, through the Electoral College.

{{story:american-revolution}}

Free from concerts, they spent months at Abbey Road Studios in [[@london|London]]. They played tapes backwards, sped voices up, and stacked many recordings on top of each other. George Martin, trained in [[@classical-western|classical music]], helped them add strings, brass and even a full orchestra.

{{story:sound-recording}}

Their 1967 album Sgt. Pepper's Lonely Hearts Club Band was planned as a whole, not just a collection of singles. Many people still think of it as one of the most important albums ever made. These experiments helped change how all pop music was [[@sound-recording|recorded]].

In 1979, an American scientist named Jon Kabat-Zinn started a programme at a medical school in Massachusetts. He took the attention exercises from Buddhist practice, left out the religion, and taught them to patients with long-lasting pain and stress. He called it Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction.

It spread fast. Today versions of it are used in hospitals, offices, sports teams and apps. In 2018, [[@delhi|Delhi]] government schools began a [[@happiness|Happiness Curriculum]], with a few minutes of mindful breathing and attention at the start of lessons.

{{story:buddha}}

In 1969 Kalam joined the new Indian Space Research Organisation, which had grown out of [[@vikram-sarabhai|Vikram Sarabhai]]'s plans. He became project director of the SLV-3, a four-stage rocket using solid fuel.

The first full launch, in 1979, failed and the rocket fell into the Bay of Bengal. Kalam later wrote that his boss, Satish Dhawan, faced the press and took the blame himself. A year later, in July 1980, SLV-3 lifted off from Sriharikota and put the Rohini satellite into orbit. Dhawan let Kalam announce the success. Learning [[@rockets-how|how rockets work]] the hard way, through failure, became part of ISRO's culture.

{{story:isro-missions}}

[[archaeologist|Archaeologists]] have found iron objects at many old sites in India. At places such as Malhar and Raja Nala ka Tila in Uttar Pradesh, iron has been dated to around 1800–1500 BCE. In southern India, people buried their dead in stone-ringed graves called megaliths, often with iron knives, swords and tools beside them.

In January 2025, Tamil Nadu's [[archaeology|archaeology]] department released a report on finds at Sivagalai and other sites, with dates from before 3000 BCE. If confirmed, that would make Indian iron-working much older than once thought. Some archaeologists welcomed the results, while others asked for more samples and testing before rewriting the textbooks.

{{story:rust}}

The Rudreshwara temple at Palampet was built in 1213 by Recharla Rudra, a general of Ganapatideva. Everyone calls it the Ramappa temple, after its chief sculptor. It is rare in India for a temple to carry an artist's name.

The temple sits on a star-shaped platform. Its pillars are carved from hard black stone and polished so smoothly that they shine. Bracket figures of dancers and musicians lean out from the tops of the pillars. Its tower is made of light bricks, and tests suggest they are porous enough to float in water. UNESCO named the temple a [[@unesco-sites|World Heritage Site]] in 2021.

{{story:marco-polo}}

The ancestors of Hawaiians were among the greatest sailors in history. In large double-hulled canoes, they crossed the Pacific with no maps or compasses. Navigators memorised where dozens of [[@constellations|stars]] rose and set. They read the shape of ocean swells, the colour of the water and the flight of birds heading home to land at dusk.

For a long time, some people doubted that such voyages were done on purpose. So in 1976, a canoe called Hōkūleʻa sailed from Hawaii to Tahiti using only traditional methods. Its navigator, Mau Piailug, came from a small Micronesian island where these skills had survived. The voyage succeeded and inspired a revival of wayfinding across the Pacific. From 2014 to 2017, Hōkūleʻa sailed around the world.

{{story:hawaii-islands}}

Around the 1st century BCE, four gateways were raised, facing north, south, east and west. Each has two square pillars and three curved beams across the top, every surface crowded with carvings. Elephants, lions, peacocks, dancers and city crowds fill the stone.

Many panels show scenes from the life of [[@buddha|the Buddha]] and from the [[@jataka-tales|Jataka tales]], stories of his past lives. In one, he is a monkey king who stretches his body across a river so his troop can escape. The craftsmanship is so fine that scholars think ivory carvers worked on it. An [[inscription|inscription]] on the south gateway records a gift from the ivory carvers of Vidisha.

{{story:ashoka}}

Buddhism reached Spiti more than a thousand years ago. Tabo Monastery was founded in 996 CE. From outside its mud walls look plain. Inside, its prayer halls are covered with wall paintings and clay statues that have survived for about a thousand years. Some people call it "the Ajanta of the Himalaya".

Key Monastery, perched on a cone-shaped hill above the Spiti River, looks like a pile of white boxes stacked up a slope. Young monks study there, and visitors are often offered butter tea. The Dhankar Monastery clings to a crumbling ridge so steeply that it has been listed among the world's most [[endangered|endangered]] monuments.

{{story:snow-leopard}}

For centuries China was the only big supplier of tea to the world, and Britain was hooked. It paid huge sums in silver, then sold opium into China to win the silver back. That ugly trade led to war.

In the 1820s, British officials learned that a tea plant was already growing wild in Assam. The Singpho people there had been brewing it for a long time. The [[@east-india-company|East India Company]] first tried Chinese plants, then switched to the tougher Assam variety. By the late 1800s, India was sending more tea to Britain than China was.

{{story:opium-wars}}

People tamed wild sheep about 10,000 years ago in West Asia, in the same region where [[@farming-began|farming began]]. Those early sheep were kept for meat and milk. Their coats were mostly hair, with a short woolly undercoat that they shed each year.

{{story:farming-began}}

Over thousands of years, farmers bred sheep with more and finer wool. By the time of [[@mesopotamia|Mesopotamia's]] [[@first-cities|first cities]], around 5,000 years ago, wool cloth was one of their most important products.
